hild_in_progress\")\r\n  600:                              or state == \"awaiting_user\"):\r\n  601:         return None\r\n  602:     expected_secs = pacing_hint(state)[0] * 60\r\n  603:     threshold = _park_threshold_secs()\r\n  604:     if expected_secs < threshold:\r\n  605:         return None\r\n  606:     return {\r\n  607:         \"park\": True,\r\n  608:         \"expected_wait_secs\": expected_secs,\r\n  609:         \"threshold_secs\": threshold,\r\n  610:         \"reason\": (f\"pacing state {state!r} expects ~{expected_secs}s of \"\r\n  611:                    f\"waiting (>= EDP_PARK_THRESHOLD_SECS={threshold}) â€” \"\r\n  612:                    \"drain your inbox once more, then pool_close_self(\"\r\n  613:                    \"park=true); the pool resumes you when a message lands.\"),\r\n  614:     }\r\n  615: \r\n  616: \r\n  617: def _enrich_wait(instr, handle: str, handle_type: str,\r\n  618:                  tick: _WaitTick | None = None,\r\n  619:                  pacing_state: str | None = None):\r\n  620:     \"\"\"v5 wake: a WAIT means 'you are a waiting parent'. Carry the\r\n  621:     deterministic heartbeat directive (handle + interval) so the\r\n  622:     activator arms/keeps its protocol cron â€” zero LLM discretion.\r\n  623: \r\n  624:     v2.1 (2026-05-22): the rationale reinforces the MCP procedure â€” on\r\n  625:     wake, drive the recipe ONLY via next_action; never hand-dispatch\r\n  626:     planners or poll the broker to route around the FSM.\r\n  627: \r\n  628:     Fix C (2026-05-24), rebuilt 2026-07-10 (backlog items 10 + 11): after\r\n  629:     enough WALL-CLOCK time with no PLAN-LEVEL PROGRESS, the rationale escalates\r\n  630:     â€” the waiting party must proactively surface a status upward rather than\r\n--- 4080-4260 ---\r\n 4080:     # same cycle). `True` counts nothing: a passing gate is not a failure.\r\n 4081:     passed: bool | None = None\r\n 4082:     # v7 P4.2 â€” DID THE REVIEWER FIX ANYTHING IN-SESSION? Stated as DATA\r\n 4083:     # (never sniffed from \"FIXED:\" prose â€” the same d77/d83 rule as `passed`).\r\n 4084:     # True stamps `fixed_inline` on the action's review_verdict, which makes\r\n 4085:     # the plan FSM emit ONE latched DISPATCH_VERIFY_LEG advisory: a cheap\r\n 4086:     # verify-only worker re-runs the recorded acceptance commands verbatim,\r\n 4087:     # closing the d74 gap (the reviewer's own fix was the one artifact in the\r\n 4088:     # batch nothing independently re-ran) without reviewer-reviews-reviewer\r\n 4089:     # regress. Default False = byte-identical for every existing caller.\r\n 4090:     fixed_inline: bool = False\r\n 4091:     # WP2 G-COMMIT: the commit hash the reviewer verified the deliverable at\r\n 4092:     # (its re-run judged THIS commit). Stored in the verdict payload as data.\r\n 4093:     commit: str | None = None\r\n 4094: \r\n 4095: \r\n 4096: def _caller_owns_action(plan_id: str, action_id: str) -> bool:\r\n 4097:     \"\"\"Does THIS shell's own handle name `plan_id:action_id`?\r\n 4098: \r\n 4099:     A pool-spawned worker's EDP_HANDLE is exactly `<plan_id>:<action_id>`. The\r\n 4100:     handle is stamped by the pool at spawn (pty_launcher), not by the shell, so\r\n 4101:     a shell cannot forge someone else's identity by editing its own request â€”\r\n 4102:     it would have to edit its own environment, which is the pool's to set.\r\n 4103: \r\n 4104:     Used to enforce d30: nobody blesses the action they themselves executed.\r\n 4105:     \"\"\"\r\n 4106:     me, _parent = _self_and_parent_addresses()\r\n 4107:     return bool(me) and me == f\"{plan_id}:{action_id}\"\r\n 4108: \r\n 4109: \r\n 4110: class RecordBranchVerdict(_ClaudeTool):\r\n 4111:     \"\"\"Record a substantive verdict â€” on a COMPREHENSION BRANCH (the neuron's\r\n 4112:     OCAK path, unchanged) or, when `plan_id` is passed, on a PLAN ACTION (s26\r\n 4113:     item 6, the reviewer's path).\r\n 4114: \r\n 4115:     Why the action path exists: d29/d30 make the reviewer's independent re-run\r\n 4116:     THE objective gate of an action, and every plan carries a reviewer leg â€” but\r\n 4117:     the verdict had nowhere to land. This tool resolved ONLY\r\n 4118:     `recipe.comprehension.branches`, so a reviewer stamping a verdict for action\r\n 4119:     `a4` (or step `s25`) got `no branch 'a4'`. It failed in WARN mode, today,\r\n 4120:     independent of role scope. s24 and s25 both hit that wall and survived only\r\n 4121:     by filing the verdict as `record_action_status` evidence.\r\n 4122: \r\n 4123:     d30 SEPARATION, enforced two independent ways:\r\n 4124:       1. This verb is NOT on the worker's surface (roles.py `_WORKER`), so the\r\n 4125:          shell that DID the work cannot call it at all. Granting it there is the\r\n 4126:          trap the s26 brief names explicitly â€” do not.\r\n 4127:       2. Even if a shell reaches it, `_caller_owns_action` REFUSES a verdict on\r\n 4128:          the caller's OWN action. Belt and braces: the role table is warn-mode\r\n 4129:          today (d14/d15), so the in-tool guard is the one that actually holds.\r\n 4130: \r\n 4131:     A verdict is a JUDGEMENT, not a status: it lands on `action.review_verdict`\r\n 4132:     and never touches `action.status` or `acceptance.actual`. The worker owns\r\n 4133:     those. That asymmetry is the whole point.\"\"\"\r\n 4134: \r\n 4135:     name = \"record_branch_verdict\"\r\n 4136:     InputModel = _BVIn\r\n 4137:     OutputModel = _Ok\r\n 4138: \r\n 4139:     async def _run(self, m: _BVIn):\r\n 4140:         if m.plan_id:\r\n 4141:             return await self._action_verdict(m)\r\n 4142:         r = self.ctx.recipes.load(m.recipe_id)\r\n 4143:         for b in r.comprehension.branches:\r\n 4144:             if b.id != m.branch_id:\r\n 4145:                 continue\r\n 4146:             if m.needs_user:\r\n 4147:                 b.status = \"needs_user_input\"\r\n 4148:                 if m.question_for_user:\r\n 4149:                     b.question = m.question_for_user\r\n 4150:                 self.ctx.recipes.save(r)\r\n 4151:                 return Tool.ok(_Ok())\r\n 4152:             v = (m.verdict or \"\").strip()\r\n 4153:             if len(v) < 40 or v.lower() in _TRIVIAL_VERDICT:\r\n 4154:                 return _precondition(\r\n 4155:                     f\"branch '{m.branch_id}' verdict is too shallow â€” \"\r\n 4156:                     \"answer THIS goal specifically (â‰¥40 chars, concrete), \"\r\n 4157:                     \"or set needs_user=True. Do not guess-and-resolve.\"\r\n 4158:                 )\r\n 4159:             b.status = \"resolved\"\r\n 4160:             b.verdict = v\r\n 4161:             self.ctx.recipes.save(r)\r\n 4162:             return Tool.ok(_Ok())\r\n 4163:         return _precondition(\r\n 4164:             f\"no branch {m.branch_id!r} in this recipe. (To stamp a verdict on \"\r\n 4165:             f\"a PLAN ACTION instead, pass plan_id=<the plan> and \"\r\n 4166:             f\"branch_id=<the action_id>.)\"\r\n 4167:         )\r\n 4168: \r\n 4169:     async def _action_verdict(self, m: _BVIn):\r\n 4170:         if not self.ctx.plans.exists(m.plan_id or \"\"):\r\n 4171:             return _precondition(f\"no plan {m.plan_id!r}\")\r\n 4172:         p = self.ctx.plans.load(m.plan_id or \"\")\r\n 4173:         a = next((x for x in p.actions if x.action_id == m.branch_id), None)\r\n 4174:         if a is None:\r\n 4175:             return _precondition(\r\n 4176:                 f\"no action {m.branch_id!r} in plan {m.plan_id!r}\")\r\n 4177:         # d30: a shell may not bless the action it itself executed.\r\n 4178:         if _caller_owns_action(m.plan_id or \"\", m.branch_id):\r\n 4179:             return _precondition(\r\n 4180:                 f\"REFUSED: {m.branch_id!r} is YOUR OWN action â€” a worker \"\r\n 4181:                 \"cannot bless its own work (d30). The verdict on an action is \"\r\n 4182:                 \"an INDEPENDENT reviewer's, recorded from a different shell. \"\r\n 4183:                 \"Record your own result with record_action_status(status, \"\r\n 4184:                 \"evidence) instead; the reviewer re-runs the gate and stamps \"\r\n 4185:                 \"the verdict.\"\r\n 4186:             )\r\n 4187:         if m.needs_user:\r\n 4188:             return _precondition(\r\n 4189:                 \"needs_user is a COMPREHENSION-BRANCH escape (it parks a \"\r\n 4190:                 \"branch for the user). An action verdict has no such state â€” \"\r\n 4191:                 \"record the verdict text, or raise the question with \"\r\n 4192:                 \"ask_above/notify_above.\"\r\n 4193:             )\r\n 4194:         v = (m.verdict or \"\").strip()\r\n 4195:         if len(v) < 40 or v.lower() in _TRIVIAL_VERDICT:\r\n 4196:             return _precondition(\r\n 4197:                 f\"action '{m.branch_id}' verdict is too shallow â€” say what you \"\r\n 4198:                 \"re-ran, what you observed, and why it passes or fails \"\r\n 4199:                 \"(â‰¥40 chars, concrete). Do not rubber-stamp.\"\r\n 4200:             )\r\n 4201:         # F35 R3b#9: `passed` is the flag the plan FSM's success boundary\r\n 4202:         # keys on (G-VERDICT). A prose fail with the flag omitted fell\r\n 4203:         # straight through to `succeeded` â€” the exact silent path the\r\n 4204:         # reopen exists to close. Explicit true/false, always.\r\n 4205:         if m.passed is None:\r\n 4206:             return _precondition(\r\n 4207:                 f\"action '{m.branch_id}' verdict requires passed=true|false \"\r\n 4208:                 \"â€” the plan's success boundary keys on this flag \"\r\n 4209:                 \"(G-VERDICT reopens a failed action; prose alone decides \"\r\n 4210:                 \"nothing). State the gate result explicitly and re-record.\"\r\n 4211:             )\r\n 4212:         me, _parent = _self_and_parent_addresses()\r\n 4213:         a.review_verdict = {\r\n 4214:             \"verdict\": v,\r\n 4215:             \"by\": me or os.environ.get(\"EDP_ROLE\", \"\").strip() or \"unknown\",\r\n 4216:             \"at\": _now().isoformat(),\r\n 4217:         }\r\n 4218:         if m.passed is not None:\r\n 4219:             a.review_verdict[\"passed\"] = m.passed\r\n 4220:         # v7 P4.2: the reviewer STATES it fixed something in-session â€” the\r\n 4221:         # stamp the plan FSM's DISPATCH_VERIFY_LEG advisory keys on.\r\n 4222:         if m.fixed_inline:\r\n 4223:             a.review_verdict[\"fixed_inline\"] = True\r\n 4224:         # WP2 G-COMMIT: the commit the reviewer's re-run judged, as data.\r\n 4225:         if m.commit:\r\n 4226:             a.review_verdict[\"commit\"] = m.commit\r\n 4227:         # W10b SEAM (b) â€” the REVIEWER's independent re-run of the acceptance\r\n 4228:         # gate (d29/d30: this re-run IS the objective gate). Counted only when\r\n 4229:         # the reviewer explicitly STATES the gate failed; never inferred from the\r\n 4230:         # prose above. `bump_verify_failure` is idempotent per DISPATCH cycle, so\r\n 4231:         # if the worker already reported this same failed cycle via\r\n 4232:         # record_action_status(status=\"failed\"), this is a no-op â€” one cycle,\r\n 4233:         # one count. That matters: worker-fails-then-reviewer-agrees is the\r\n 4234:         # dual gate working, not a stuck action.\r\n 4235:         if m.passed is False:\r\n 4236:             bump_verify_failure(a)\r\n 4237:         self.ctx.plans.append_worklog(p.plan_id, {\r\n 4238:             \"kind\": \"action_verdict_recorded\",\r\n 4239:             \"action_id\": m.branch_id,\r\n 4240:             \"agent_role\": os.environ.get(\"EDP_ROLE\", \"\").strip() or \"unknown\",\r\n 4241:             \"passed\": m.passed,\r\n 4242:             \"detail\": v[:200],\r\n 4243:         })\r\n 4244:         self.ctx.plans.save(p)\r\n 4245:         return Tool.ok(_Ok())\r\n 4246: \r\n 4247: \r\n 4248: class _OutIn(BaseModel):\r\n 4249:     recipe_id: str\r\n 4250:     description: str\r\n 4251:     verification: str\r\n 4252: \r\n 4253: \r\n 4254: class RecordOutcome(_ClaudeTool):\r\n 4255:     name = \"record_outcome\"\r\n 4256:     InputModel = _OutIn\r\n 4257:     OutputModel = _Ok\r\n 4258: \r\n 4259:     async def _run(self, m: _OutIn):\r\n 4260:         from ..schemas import Outcome\r\n--- 14520-14720 ---\r\n14520:     type: str\r\n14521:     ids: dict = {}\r\n14522:     patch: dict = {}\r\n14523:     # WP1: recorded user_gate_answer ref (answer_id) for a gated patch â€”\r\n14524:     # e.g. flipping a spawn_planner step to done without a terminal-\r\n14525:     # succeeded plan requires one validating against\r\n14526:     # \"G-STEP:<recipe_id>:<step_id>\".\r\n14527:     override_ref: str | None = None\r\n14528: \r\n14529: \r\n14530: class _ObjResultOut(BaseModel):\r\n14531:     result: Any         # the create/update result dict\r\n14532: \r\n14533: \r\n14534: # â”€â”€ DESIGN-v6 W4: per-role / per-object-type object-CRUD guard â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€\r\n14535: # roles.py owns the PURE policy (crud_scope_violation); the I/O (env read +\r\n14536: # worklog write + warn-vs-enforce) lives here, next to the tools it guards.\r\n14537: def _role_scope_worklog_key() -> str | None:\r\n14538:     \"\"\"The worklog key under which this shell's role_scope_violation is\r\n14539:     recorded. Reuses _self_and_parent_addresses so the mapping stays in ONE\r\n14540:     place (mirrors _sender_plan_id but covers every scoped role).\r\n14541: \r\n14542:     - planner  â†’ its dash plan_id (the plan it owns).\r\n14543:     - worker   â†’ its plan_id (the handle prefix before the last ':').\r\n14544:     - BARE-HANDLE roles (reviewer / specialist / consult / curiosity) â†’\r\n14545:       THE HANDLE ITSELF. Their handles carry\r\n14546:       no ':' (`review-<neuron_id>-<hex8>`, `consult-<hex8>`, â€¦), so\r\n14547:       _self_and_parent_addresses returns (handle, None) and there is no plan to\r\n14548:       attribute the event to. This used to mean `if pid:` was False and the\r\n14549:       event was SILENTLY DROPPED â€” so \"zero violations\" from those six roles was\r\n14550:       indistinguishable from \"never instrumented\", while four of the seven known\r\n14551:       guideâ†”toolset gaps live in exactly those roles. The d14/d15 flip gate (\"a\r\n14552:       zero-violation recipe\") was therefore measuring nothing for them. Keying\r\n14553:       the trail by the handle records the event under a shell-scoped worklog\r\n14554:       (readable with `read_worklog(<handle>)`); no plan.json exists there, so\r\n14555:       `plans.exists(handle)` stays False and nothing mistakes it for a plan.\r\n14556:     - neuron / a truly bare shell (no EDP_HANDLE at all) â†’ None. It is\r\n14557:       unconstrained by design (toolset_for_role returns None), so it has no\r\n14558:       off-scope calls to record.\r\n14559:     \"\"\"\r\n14560:     role = os.environ.get(\"EDP_ROLE\", \"\").strip()\r\n14561:     me, parent = _self_and_parent_addresses()\r\n14562:     if role == \"planner\":\r\n14563:         return me       # dash plan_id (the plan the planner owns)\r\n14564:     return parent or me  # worker: plan_id prefix; bare handle: the handle\r\n14565: \r\n14566: \r\n14567: def record_role_scope_violation(ctx, tool_name: str,\r\n14568:                                 object_type: str | None = None) -> str:\r\n14569:     \"\"\"Append a role_scope_violation worklog event for the current shell and\r\n14570:     return the active EDP_ROLE_SCOPE mode ('warn'|'enforce', default\r\n14571:     'enforce' â€” DESIGN-v7 P0 flipped it; warn stays as the diagnostic\r\n14572:     opt-out).\r\n14573: \r\n14574:     Shared by the build_mcp warn-mode seam (an off-SET tool CALL) and the\r\n14575:     object-CRUD guard (an off-OBJECT-TYPE mutation). The write is best-effort\r\n14576:     and advisory: a failed write NEVER blocks the call. Every role with an\r\n14577:     EDP_HANDLE is now logged (see _role_scope_worklog_key) â€” the observability\r\n14578:     prerequisite for any future EDP_ROLE_SCOPE=enforce decision.\"\"\"\r\n14579:     role = os.environ.get(\"EDP_ROLE\", \"\").strip() or \"unknown\"\r\n14580:     mode = (os.environ.get(\"EDP_ROLE_SCOPE\", \"enforce\").strip()\r\n14581:             or \"enforce\")\r\n14582:     key = _role_scope_worklog_key()\r\n14583:     if key:\r\n14584:         entry: dict = {\r\n14585:             \"kind\": \"role_scope_violation\", \"agent_role\": role,\r\n14586:             \"tool\": tool_name, \"mode\": mode,\r\n14587:             # the shell that made the off-scope call. Load-bearing for the\r\n14588:             # bare-handle roles, whose trail is keyed by handle rather than by\r\n14589:             # a plan â€” without it the event cannot be attributed to a shell.\r\n14590:             \"handle\": os.environ.get(\"EDP_HANDLE\", \"\").strip() or None,\r\n14591:         }\r\n14592:         if object_type is not None:\r\n14593:             entry[\"object_type\"] = object_type\r\n14594:         try:\r\n14595:             ctx.plans.append_worklog(key, entry)\r\n14596:         except Exception:\r\n14597:             pass  # advisory only â€” never let logging break the tool call\r\n14598:     return mode\r\n14599: \r\n14600: \r\n14601: def _effect_role_violation(effect: dict | None) -> str | None:\r\n14602:     \"\"\"F37#10 â€” an EffectSpec executes with the INITIATING shell's authority.\r\n14603: \r\n14604:     The composed action must be a verb the caller's own role holds\r\n14605:     (ROLE_TOOLSETS): a worker whose surface lacks `broker_send` cannot\r\n14606:     regain it by wrapping it in observe(effect=...) / register_rule. Read\r\n14607:     from the server-process env (unforgeable by the model). Returns a\r\n14608:     refuse-message, or None when authorized (incl. the unconstrained\r\n14609:     operator console).\"\"\"\r\n14610:     if not effect:\r\n14611:         return None\r\n14612:     from .roles import toolset_for_role\r\n14613:     role = os.environ.get(\"EDP_ROLE\", \"\").strip() or None\r\n14614:     allowed = toolset_for_role(role)\r\n14615:     if allowed is None:\r\n14616:         return None  # operator console / unconstrained shell\r\n14617:     action = str(effect.get(\"action\", \"\")).strip()\r\n14618:     if action and action not in allowed:\r\n14619:         return (\r\n14620:             f\"effect action {action!r} is outside role {role!r}'s tool \"\r\n14621:             \"surface â€” an effect fires with YOUR authority, so it may only \"\r\n14622:             \"compose verbs your role already holds. Route it through the \"\r\n14623:             \"role that owns the verb (e.g. notify_above to your parent).\")\r\n14624:     return None\r\n14625: \r\n14626: \r\n14627: def _guard_object_crud(ctx, tool_name: str, object_type: str,\r\n14628:                        ids: dict | None = None):\r\n14629:     \"\"\"The in-tool CRUD guard. Returns a ToolError to REFUSE (enforce mode +\r\n14630:     off-scope) or None to PROCEED. The on-role path (e.g. a planner mutating\r\n14631:     its own plan/action) returns None immediately with NO event and ALWAYS\r\n14632:     succeeds. An off-scope call is ALWAYS recorded as a role_scope_violation\r\n14633:     first; under EDP_ROLE_SCOPE=warn (the shipped Phase-1 default, d15) it\r\n14634:     then PROCEEDS, under enforce it is refused with a _precondition message\r\n14635:     naming the role and its allowed object-types.\r\n14636: \r\n14637:     F37#11 (2026-08-18) â€” OWNERSHIP, not just type: the planner's CRUD grant\r\n14638:     is \"mutate its OWN plan\" (the W4-remediation comment in roles.py), but\r\n14639:     the type-only check let a planner update/delete another plan's objects.\r\n14640:     When `ids` names a plan_id that is not the caller's own plan, the call\r\n14641:     is a violation under the same warn/enforce policy.\"\"\"\r\n14642:     from .roles import crud_scope_violation\r\n14643:     role = os.environ.get(\"EDP_ROLE\", \"\").strip() or None\r\n14644:     msg = crud_scope_violation(role, object_type)\r\n14645:     if msg is None and role == \"planner\" and object_type in (\"plan\", \"action\"):\r\n14646:         own_plan, _parent = _self_and_parent_addresses()\r\n14647:         target = str((ids or {}).get(\"plan_id\") or \"\").strip()\r\n14648:         if own_plan and target and target != own_plan:\r\n14649:             msg = (\r\n14650:                 f\"role 'planner' (plan {own_plan!r}) may not mutate \"\r\n14651:                 f\"{object_type!r} objects of plan {target!r} â€” a planner's \"\r\n14652:                 \"CRUD reach is its OWN plan only. If another plan's state \"\r\n14653:                 \"is wrong, tell the neuron (notify_above).\"\r\n14654:             )\r\n14655:     if msg is None:\r\n14656:         return None  # on-scope (or unconstrained shell) â€” proceed silently\r\n14657:     mode = record_role_scope_violation(ctx, tool_name, object_type=object_type)\r\n14658:     if mode != \"warn\":\r\n14659:         # F37#12: only the exact opt-out 'warn' proceeds â€” an unknown mode\r\n14660:         # value fails CLOSED as enforce, never open as warn.\r\n14661:         return _precondition(\r\n14662:             f\"role-scope refused: {msg} (EDP_ROLE_SCOPE=enforce)\")\r\n14663:     return None  # warn â€” logged above, now proceed\r\n14664: \r\n14665: \r\n14666: class CreateObject(_ClaudeTool):\r\n14667:     \"\"\"Create a domain object. `type` + `fields`. e.g.\r\n14668:     create_object(type='plan', fields={'recipe_id':'r','step_id':'s1',\r\n14669:     'shape':'linear-build','goal':'â€¦'}); create_object(type='action',\r\n14670:     fields={'plan_id':'p','action_id':'a1','description':'â€¦',\r\n14671:     'specialization':'â€¦'}). Delegates to the object's creation\r\n14672:     invariants (spec resolution, comprehension gate, â€¦) â€” so the rules\r\n14673:     are enforced exactly once. Inspect-only objects refuse create.\"\"\"\r\n14674: \r\n14675:     name = \"create_object\"\r\n14676:     InputModel = _CreateObjIn\r\n14677:     OutputModel = _ObjResultOut\r\n14678: \r\n14679:     async def _run(self, m: _CreateObjIn):\r\n14680:         from ..objects import ObjectError, create_object\r\n14681:         refuse = _guard_object_crud(self.ctx, self.name, m.type)\r\n14682:         if refuse is not None:\r\n14683:             return refuse\r\n14684:         try:\r\n14685:             res = await create_object(self.ctx, m.type, m.fields)\r\n14686:         except ObjectError as e:\r\n14687:             return _precondition(str(e))\r\n14688:         return Tool.ok(_ObjResultOut(result=res))\r\n14689: \r\n14690: \r\n14691: class UpdateObject(_ClaudeTool):\r\n14692:     \"\"\"Patch a domain object through its encapsulated validation: `type` +\r\n14693:     `ids` + `patch` (e.g. type='action',\r\n14694:     ids={'plan_id':'p','action_id':'a1'}, patch={'status':'done',\r\n14695:     'evidence':'â€¦'}). An action `done` patch is a **pure write (d30): it\r\n14696:     records status + evidence, runs NO acceptance check, and lands as `done`\r\n14697:     awaiting the worker->reviewer chain**; patching `{'verify':{â€¦}}` CORRECTS\r\n14698:     a wrong acceptance.verify (data the worker/reviewer re-run in-shell) and is\r\n14699:     allowed while dispatching. Invalid transitions are refused by the object; inspect-only\r\n14700:     objects refuse update. Worked examples (outcome / recipe-close / etc.):\r\n14701:     get_guide('architecture-vocabulary').\"\"\"\r\n14702: \r\n14703:     name = \"update_object\"\r\n14704:     InputModel = _UpdateObjIn\r\n14705:     OutputModel = _ObjResultOut\r\n14706: \r\n14707:     async def _run(self, m: _UpdateObjIn):\r\n14708:         from ..objects import ObjectError, update_object\r\n14709:         refuse = _guard_object_crud(self.ctx, self.name, m.type, ids=m.ids)\r\n14710:         if refuse is not None:\r\n14711:             return refuse\r\n14712:         try:\r\n14713:             res = await update_object(self.ctx, m.type, m.ids, m.patch,\r\n14714:                                       override_ref=m.override_ref)\r\n14715:         except ObjectError as e:\r\n14716:             return _precondition(str(e))\r\n14717:         return Tool.ok(_ObjResultOut(result=res))\r\n14718: \r\n14719: \r\n14720: class _DeleteObjIn(BaseModel):\r\n--- 15170-15280 ---\r\n15170:     \"specialist\": (\r\n15171:         \"call check_inbox() and if there is an answer or update task, act \"\r\n15172:         \"on it; otherwise end the turn and wait.\"),\r\n15173:     \"acceptor\": (\r\n15174:         \"call check_inbox() and if there is an answer to your ask_above, \"\r\n15175:         \"resume the acceptance pass with it; otherwise end the turn and \"\r\n15176:         \"wait.\"),\r\n15177: }\r\n15178: \r\n15179: \r\n15180: def _wiring_heartbeat_minutes(role: str) -> int:\r\n15181:     from ..cadence import heartbeat_secs\r\n15182:     if role in (\"neuron\", \"planner\"):\r\n15183:         return max(1, heartbeat_secs() // 60)\r\n15184:     if role in (\"worker\", \"reviewer\"):\r\n15185:         try:\r\n15186:             return max(1, int(os.environ.get(\"EDP_WORKER_HEARTBEAT_MIN\", \"5\")))\r\n15187:         except ValueError:\r\n15188:             return 5\r\n15189:     if role == \"specialist\":\r\n15190:         return 10\r\n15191:     return 5    # curiosity / acceptor\r\n15192: \r\n15193: \r\n15194: class _ArmWiringIn(BaseModel):\r\n15195:     # Only the NEURON (no EDP_HANDLE) must pass this: its recipe_id. Every\r\n15196:     # spawned role resolves everything from env.\r\n15197:     handle: str | None = None\r\n15198: \r\n15199: \r\n15200: class _ArmWiringOut(BaseModel):\r\n15201:     subscription_id: str\r\n15202:     spec: str\r\n15203:     monitor_cmd: str        # run THIS under the harness Monitor tool, once\r\n15204:     cron_expr: str          # arm CronCreate with exactly this exprâ€¦\r\n15205:     cron_prompt: str        # â€¦and exactly this prompt\r\n15206:     reused: bool            # True â†’ identical wiring already armed: do NOT\r\n15207:                             # start a second Monitor; re-arm the cron only if\r\n15208:                             # you know yours is gone\r\n15209:     note: str\r\n15210: \r\n15211: \r\n15212: class ArmWiring(_ClaudeTool):\r\n15213:     \"\"\"ONE call arms your whole wake plane. Composes your role's default\r\n15214:     reactive subscription server-side (no rx DSL to learn), persists it, and\r\n15215:     returns verbatim: the `monitor_cmd` to run under the harness Monitor\r\n15216:     tool (your push wake â€” events arrive as TOOL output) and the CronCreate\r\n15217:     `cron_expr` + `cron_prompt` for your heartbeat backstop (numbers\r\n15218:     resolved, nothing to substitute). Run both, then boot on. Idempotent:\r\n15219:     re-arming returns reused=True with the same command â€” one logical\r\n15220:     subscription, one Monitor. Neuron: pass handle=<recipe_id>; spawned\r\n15221:     roles pass nothing.\"\"\"\r\n15222: \r\n15223:     name = \"arm_wiring\"\r\n15224:     InputModel = _ArmWiringIn\r\n15225:     OutputModel = _ArmWiringOut\r\n15226: \r\n15227:     async def _run(self, m: _ArmWiringIn):\r\n15228:         from ..cadence import RECONCILE_LOOP_CRON_PROMPT\r\n15229:         role = os.environ.get(\"EDP_ROLE\", \"\").strip()\r\n15230:         me, _parent = _self_and_parent_addresses()\r\n15231:         if not me:\r\n15232:             me = (m.handle or \"\").strip()\r\n15233:         if not role:\r\n15234:             # A role-less shell arming wiring is the neuron's foreground seat.\r\n15235:             role = \"neuron\"\r\n15236:         if role not in _WIRING_SPECS:\r\n15237:             return _precondition(\r\n15238:                 f\"arm_wiring: no wiring profile for role {role!r} â€” known \"\r\n15239:                 f\"roles: {sorted(_WIRING_SPECS)}.\")\r\n15240:         if not me:\r\n15241:             return _precondition(\r\n15242:                 \"arm_wiring: no handle resolves â€” the neuron's foreground \"\r\n15243:                 \"seat must pass handle=<recipe_id>; spawned shells need \"\r\n15244:                 \"EDP_HANDLE set (report a spawn-env defect upward if it \"\r\n15245:                 \"is not).\")\r\n15246: \r\n15247:         bindings: dict = {\"me\": me}\r\n15248:         if role == \"planner\":\r\n15249:             handle = os.environ.get(\"EDP_HANDLE\", \"\").strip() or me\r\n15250:             recipe_id = handle.rsplit(\":\", 1)[0] if \":\" in handle else handle\r\n15251:             bindings.update({\"plan_id\": me, \"recipe_id\": recipe_id})\r\n15252: \r\n15253:         spec = _WIRING_SPECS[role]\r\n15254:         # F36 R4#11: the sanitize+truncate SID was not injective â€” `a:b` vs\r\n15255:         # `a_b`, or two long handles sharing a 60-char prefix, collided and\r\n15256:         # overwrote/deleted each other's wiring. A hash of the FULL handle\r\n15257:         # makes the identity collision-resistant; the readable prefix stays.\r\n15258:         import hashlib as _hl\r\n15259:         sid = (\"sub-wiring-\" + re.sub(r\"[^A-Za-z0-9._-]\", \"_\", me)[:48]\r\n15260:                + \"-\" + _hl.sha256(me.encode(\"utf-8\")).hexdigest()[:8])\r\n15261:         res = await ObserveStream(self.ctx)._run(_ObserveIn(\r\n15262:             spec=spec, bindings=bindings, subscription_id=sid, owner=me))\r\n15263:         if not getattr(res, \"ok\", False):\r\n15264:             return res\r\n15265:         obs = res.data if isinstance(res.data, dict) else \\\r\n15266:             res.data.model_dump()\r\n15267:         # F36 R4#5: subscription FILES are not a live Monitor. reused=True\r\n15268:         # is honest only while the driver's heartbeat sidecar is fresh â€” a\r\n15269:         # dead Monitor's leftovers used to answer \"don't start another\" and\r\n15270:         # leave the shell deaf. Stale/absent heartbeat â†’ tell the caller to\r\n15271:         # START the returned monitor_cmd (the files are reused; the process\r\n15272:         # is not).\r\n15273:         reused = bool(obs.get(\"reused\"))\r\n15274:         if reused:\r\n15275:             _root = self.ctx.recipes.root.parent / \".reactive\"\r\n15276:             _hb = _root / f\"{sid}.spec.hb\"\r\n15277:             _fresh = False\r\n15278:             try:\r\n15279:                 _hb_rec = json.loads(_hb.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\r\n15280:                 _age = (_now() - datetime.fromisoformat(\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
