{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_65","type":"agent_message","text":"[\n  {\n    \"finding\": \"Delegation policy and model authorization are loaded from files writable by the very agents they constrain.\",\n    \"evidence\": \"claude/src/edp_claude/tools/bridge.py:147-165 loads routes from <EDP_AGENT_HOME>/.bridge.json on each call; claude/src/edp_claude/tools/_tools.py:9633-9648 loads the model registry from EDP_AGENT_HOME/models.json; edp-pool/src/edp_pool/main.py:49 and edp-pool/src/edp_pool/spawner.py:240-275 launch agents with that directory as their working tree and skip-permissions enabled.\",\n    \"severity\": \"high\",\n    \"target\": \"F38 delegation route/model authorization\",\n    \"suggested_fix\": \"Move policy files outside agent-writable working trees and enforce operator-owned filesystem permissions or verify a signed/hash-pinned snapshot. Refuse delegation and spawning if policy provenance or immutability cannot be established.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A prompt-injected worker edits .bridge.json to authorize an expensive delegate for itself, or a planner adds an arbitrary model to models.json. Its next delegate or spawn request passes the new guards, causing unauthorized execution and spend.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"finding\": \"Planner plan-ownership enforcement covers generic CRUD but not native planner mutation verbs.\",\n    \"evidence\": \"claude/src/edp_claude/tools/_tools.py:14627-14663 enforces own-plan access only in _guard_object_crud; claude/src/edp_claude/tools/roles.py:280-297 grants planners native create_plan, record_plan, add_action, record_action_status, and record_step_result verbs; claude/src/edp_claude/tools/_tools.py:3374-3397 and 3742-3749 accept a supplied plan without deriving or checking ownership; claude/src/edp_claude/tools/_tools.py:6164-6191 has ownership checks for reviewers and workers but none for planners.\",\n    \"severity\": \"high\",\n    \"target\": \"F37 planner ownership guard\",\n    \"suggested_fix\": \"Apply one server-side caller-to-plan ownership helper to every native and generic plan mutator. Derive plan_id from the authenticated handle wherever possible and reject supplied foreign identifiers.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Planner recipe-A:s1 invokes add_action or record_action_status against recipe-B-s1. The request is accepted and corrupts the other recipe, potentially marking dependencies skipped or complete without their work occurring.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"finding\": \"Worker ownership is checked only at plan granularity, so a worker can mutate unrelated actions in its own plan.\",\n    \"evidence\": \"claude/src/edp_claude/tools/_tools.py:6164-6191 compares only the handle's recipe prefix with plan_id; claude/src/edp_claude/tools/_tools.py:6199-6205 accepts a grounding echo from the caller without tying it to the target action; mutations are persisted at 6347-6355 and 6431-6443. claude/tests/test_f37_round5.py:123-139 tests only a foreign-plan attempt.\",\n    \"severity\": \"high\",\n    \"target\": \"F37 record_action_status worker ownership\",\n    \"suggested_fix\": \"Authorize the exact dispatched action. For batches, permit only the batch head and actions whose server-recorded batch_owner is that head. Bind grounding evidence to the target action rather than merely the caller handle.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Worker plan:a1 calls record_action_status for unrelated same-plan action a2 with status skipped. The FSM treats a2's dependency as satisfied and advances even though no worker assigned to a2 completed it.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"finding\": \"The findings contract accepts prose-wrapped output and silently converts malformed findings into an empty clean result.\",\n    \"evidence\": \"claude/src/edp_claude/tools/bridge.py:65-70 requires only a JSON array, but 278-298 slices from the first '[' to the last ']' and filters invalid elements instead of rejecting the response. claude/tests/test_bridge.py:147-166 explicitly pins acceptance of preamble and trailing prose. claude/src/edp_claude/tools/_tools.py:9853-9862 treats any persisted challenge line as satisfying G-CHALLENGE.\",\n    \"severity\": \"high\",\n    \"target\": \"F38 findings contract and challenge gate\",\n    \"suggested_fix\": \"Parse content.strip() as the entire JSON document and validate every element against a strict schema with nonempty typed fields. Reject the complete response if any prose, unknown shape, or malformed entry is present; never filter invalid entries into [].\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Injected target text induces the delegate to answer \\\"APPROVED []\\\" or [{\\\"finding\\\":123}]. The parser returns [], the challenge is persisted and adjudicated, and the gate closes even though the mandated adversarial review did not produce a conforming result.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"finding\": \"Spawn environment redaction is a denylist with broad prefix exemptions, leaving common credentials available to prompt-injected agents.\",\n    \"evidence\": \"edp-pool/src/edp_pool/pty_launcher.py:439-463 recognizes only a small marker set and exempts all EDP_, ANTHROPIC_, and CLAUDE_ variables; edp-pool/src/edp_pool/pty_launcher.py:491 copies the resulting ambient environment. edp-pool/tests/test_f37_spawn_secret_hygiene.py:25-36 pins preservation of EDP_BRIDGE_TOKEN_THING.\",\n    \"severity\": \"high\",\n    \"target\": \"F38 delegation/spawn redaction\",\n    \"suggested_fix\": \"Construct child environments from a positive allowlist of required variables. Make secret-name and credential-source detection override namespace exemptions, and explicitly cover access keys, PATs, cookies, sessions, auth configs, and provider credentials.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"The operator environment contains AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID, GITHUB_PAT, DOCKER_AUTH_CONFIG, or EDP_OPENAI_API_KEY. A prompt-injected spawned agent reads and discloses it because none is removed by the current rules.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"finding\": \"Budget accounting fails open when audit persistence fails or an audit line is malformed.\",\n    \"evidence\": \"claude/src/edp_claude/tools/bridge.py:308-316 catches audit append failures and continues; claude/src/edp_claude/tools/_tools.py:10281-10296 silently skips malformed JSON lines and reports audit errors only for file-level I/O failures; claude/src/edp_claude/tools/_tools.py:10340-10344 gates solely on successfully aggregated cost.\",\n    \"severity\": \"high\",\n    \"target\": \"F38/F39 delegate budget attribution\",\n    \"suggested_fix\": \"Reserve budget durably before the paid call and settle it afterward, or latch an audit-degraded state that blocks further paid operations. Treat malformed, truncated, missing-after-use, or unwritable audit records as incomplete accounting and fail the budget gate closed.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A provider call succeeds while the audit directory is full or unwritable, or its JSONL record is torn. The result is returned but its cost disappears; later calls see no audit error and continue beyond the configured cap.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"finding\": \"Paid calls from bare-role handles are deliberately unattributed, yet recipe budget gates ignore their cost and can report complete accounting.\",\n    \"evidence\": \"claude/src/edp_claude/tools/_tools.py:10248-10263 maps curiosity and acceptor handles to no recipe; 10297-10304 places their cost in an unattributed bucket excluded from recipe cost; 10340-10344 gates on recipe-attributed cost only; 10444-10453 can state that delegate actuals are complete despite nonzero unattributed cost.\",\n    \"severity\": \"high\",\n    \"target\": \"F39 _caller_recipe budget attribution\",\n    \"suggested_fix\": \"Stamp an immutable recipe identifier or lineage token into every spawn and audit record and validate it server-side. Until attribution is certain, make nonzero paid unattributed cost render accounting incomplete and block affected recipe or fleet budgets.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A curiosity process spawned for a capped recipe makes a $10 HTTP consultation. Its bare curiosity-<id> handle produces no recipe attribution, so a $5 recipe budget still shows $0 and remains open.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"finding\": \"Unique temporary filenames do not prevent concurrent threads.json updates from losing mappings.\",\n    \"evidence\": \"claude/src/edp_claude/tools/sol_bridge.py:247-273 performs an unlocked read-modify-replace of the entire threads.json file; claude/src/edp_claude/tools/bridge.py:385-396 permits two concurrent CLI turns. Unique temporary paths prevent temp-name collision but not stale-snapshot overwrites.\",\n    \"severity\": \"high\",\n    \"target\": \"F38 unique-file thread-state fix\",\n    \"suggested_fix\": \"Protect the complete read-modify-replace transaction with an interprocess lock or store each caller mapping independently in per-key files or a transactional database.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Two callers concurrently load an empty map, each adds its own thread ID, and each replaces threads.json. The last replace erases the first mapping, so that caller loses conversation context and may repeat work and spend.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"finding\": \"Broker provenance stamping preserves a caller-supplied _sender object instead of overwriting it.\",\n    \"evidence\": \"claude/src/edp_claude/store/http_broker.py:33-45 stamps provenance only when _sender is absent; claude/src/edp_claude/tools/_tools.py:11346-11351 merges arbitrary ask_above body fields into the outgoing message; claude/src/edp_claude/tools/_tools.py:12336-12341 tells recipients that _sender is server-stamped and trustworthy.\",\n    \"severity\": \"medium\",\n    \"target\": \"F37 HTTP broker provenance stamp\",\n    \"suggested_fix\": \"Remove any incoming _sender field and overwrite it unconditionally at the trusted broker seam. Keep client metadata in a separate explicitly untrusted namespace.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A compromised worker sends ask_above with _sender claiming an acceptor or planner identity. The broker retains it, and the recipient follows instructions under the documented assumption that the provenance is trustworthy.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"finding\": \"Delegate override authorization is role-wide rather than scoped to the requested task class.\",\n    \"evidence\": \"claude/src/edp_claude/tools/_tools.py:9651-9677 accepts an explicit delegate if it appears under any route for the caller role, without requiring it to match the resolved role-and-task-class route. claude/tests/test_f38_round6.py:40-59 tests only a delegate absent from every route for that role.\",\n    \"severity\": \"medium\",\n    \"target\": \"F38 delegate route authorization\",\n    \"suggested_fix\": \"Resolve the exact and wildcard route for the supplied role and task class first. Allow an override only if it equals that resolved delegate or appears in an explicit allowlist keyed by both role and task class.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Routes authorize cheap-model for worker:tests and expensive-model for worker:codegen. A worker submits task_class=tests with delegate=expensive-model; authorization succeeds merely because that delegate appears elsewhere under worker.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"finding\": \"Reactive wiring reuse compares the spec and bindings but not the effect definition, so a changed effect can silently reuse stale behavior.\",\n    \"evidence\": \"claude/src/edp_claude/tools/_tools.py:14802-14833 validates only that the effect file exists; 14969-14987 and 15034-15049 reuse matching subscriptions without rewriting the effect file.\",\n    \"severity\": \"medium\",\n    \"target\": \"F39 arm_wiring role profiles and effect authorization\",\n    \"suggested_fix\": \"Canonicalize and compare the complete effect document, including role and owner identity, before reuse. Update the spec, bindings, and effect as one atomic versioned unit or allocate a new subscription ID.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A subscription is re-armed with identical event bindings but a corrected or more restrictive effect. The call reports reuse while the driver continues executing the old effect.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"finding\": \"Active reactive subscriptions can be garbage-collected after seven days because reuse does not renew their lease and GC ignores driver heartbeat.\",\n    \"evidence\": \"claude/src/edp_claude/tools/_tools.py:14962-14987 deliberately avoids rewriting an identical reused spec; 14854-14899 deletes indexed subscriptions after the spec-age TTL without checking the heartbeat; claude/src/edp_claude/reactive/driver.py:883-903 exits when its spec disappears. claude/tests/test_f25_reopen_and_wiring_heal.py:125-140 covers only a 100-hour-old entry, below the seven-day boundary.\",\n    \"severity\": \"medium\",\n    \"target\": \"F39 arm_wiring lifecycle and unique-file GC\",\n    \"suggested_fix\": \"Treat a fresh heartbeat and live owner PID as a renewable lease, refresh metadata on successful reuse, and garbage-collect only subscriptions proven inactive.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A planner and its driver remain healthy for more than seven days. Another observe call runs GC, deletes the aged spec, and the live driver exits, leaving the planner unable to receive events.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"finding\": \"The newly wired acceptor cannot use its documented upward communication verbs because its bare handle has no derivable parent.\",\n    \"evidence\": \"claude/.claude/commands/acceptor.md:6-13 assigns acceptor-<hex> and expects ask_above replies through wiring; claude/src/edp_claude/tools/_tools.py:11154-11166 derives no parent for a bare handle; 11332-11338 and 11426-11431 reject ask_above and notify_above when no parent exists. claude/tests/test_f39_acceptor_wiring.py:40-50 tests wiring construction but not an upward round trip.\",\n    \"severity\": \"medium\",\n    \"target\": \"F39 acceptor wiring\",\n    \"suggested_fix\": \"Stamp immutable parent-handle and recipe identifiers at spawn time and use them for upward routing while retaining the bare handle for the acceptor's own inbox. Add an end-to-end ask/reply test.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"An acceptor encounters ambiguous evidence and follows its role card by calling ask_above. The verb returns no-parent, forcing it to guess or stall; final acceptance can then be wrong or repeatedly respawn after the latch expires.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"finding\": \"A crash between slot-directory creation and PID-file creation leaves a delegation slot that the reaper never removes.\",\n    \"evidence\": \"claude/src/edp_claude/tools/bridge.py:447-468 creates the slot directory before separately writing PID and nonce files; 469-484 catches failure to read a missing PID file without deleting or aging out that slot.\",\n    \"severity\": \"medium\",\n    \"target\": \"F38 delegation slot lock and Windows PID handling\",\n    \"suggested_fix\": \"Publish ownership metadata atomically with acquisition, or treat missing and malformed metadata as an orphan after a short creation grace period and reclaim it through a nonce-safe rename/removal protocol.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"With EDP_BRIDGE_CLI_MAX=1, the MCP process dies immediately after mkdir but before writing pid. Every later delegation waits until timeout because the slot exists and is never classified as stale.\"\n  }\n]"}}
